## 2026-08-29 - US-001: Set conflicted flag after rebase-then-merge failure

Completed. src/merge.ts mergeWave's i>0 branch previously only set
mergingResult.merged from mergeBranch's result after a successful
rebase, leaving conflicted:false even when the merge itself failed.
Mirrored the i===0 branch's handling: after mergeBranch runs,
conflicted is set to !mergeSuccess, and on failure the strategy is
respected (stop sets stoppedAt and breaks, respawn/continue skips
via continue) -- same as the rebase-failure branch above it.

Gotcha: a rebase that succeeds always leaves the branch as a
fast-forward descendant of the base, so mergeBranch immediately
afterward is always a fast-forward and cannot fail via real content
conflicts or commit hooks (fast-forward merges create no commit, so
hooks never fire). To exercise this path in a real-git integration
test, added installMergeFailingGit() in test/merge.test.ts: it
prepends a fake `git` shell script to PATH that fails only
`git merge <specific-branch>` and passes every other git invocation
through to the real binary via an absolute path captured before PATH
is modified. This lets rebaseBranch complete normally while forcing
the subsequent mergeBranch call to fail deterministically, without
mocking any internal JS.

Added 2 new tests to test/merge.test.ts (5 assertions): one verifies
conflicted:true/merged:false with strategy:respawn (continues past
the failure), one verifies strategy:stop sets stoppedAt with
conflicted:true. Verified both tests fail against the pre-fix code
(4 failing assertions) and pass after the fix.

