## 2026-08-29 - US-003: Defer markDone until after merge

Completed. In runWavesIsolated (src/orchestrator.ts), the exitCode===0
branch previously called markDone/checkTicket/writeProgress/
commitProgress immediately after the agent finished, before mergeWave
ran. Moved that bookkeeping to after mergeWave: agent success now only
logs "awaiting merge" and records the ticket in a ticketsByNum map plus
successfulBranches. After mergeWave returns, its `merged` array (one
MergeResult per attempted branch) is walked and markDone/checkTicket
are called only for branches where `merged:true`; branches that failed
to merge (or were never attempted because a 'stop' strategy broke the
loop early) are left untouched on disk, so the ticket's status stays
ready-for-agent and findReadyTickets won't unblock its dependents in a
later wave. waveCompleted/totalCompleted increments moved to the
merge-confirmed branch too, so a wave where every agent succeeded but
every merge failed is correctly treated as "no tickets completed" and
stops the run.

Gotcha (real bug uncovered, not just a test artifact): gating ticket
status on mergeWave's actual result exposed a pre-existing bug in
rebaseBranch (src/merge.ts) that silently broke every real multi-branch
wave. rebaseBranch did `git checkout <branch>` in the main repoRoot
before rebasing, but for waves with 2+ successful tickets each ticket's
branch is still checked out in its own worktree (worktree cleanup is
US-004, not done yet) -- git refuses to check out a branch that's
already checked out in another worktree ("fatal: 'x' is already used by
worktree at ..."), so the rebase always failed and the branch was always
marked conflicted. Previously this was invisible because markDone ran
regardless of merge outcome. Fixed by adding an optional worktreePath
param to rebaseBranch/mergeWave's branch entries: when present, the
rebase runs with cwd=worktreePath (git rebase onto, no checkout needed
since the branch is already checked out there) instead of checking the
branch out in repoRoot. mergeBranch doesn't need this since `git merge
<branch>` never requires checking the branch itself out. orchestrator.ts
now threads worktree.worktreePath through successfulBranches into
mergeWave. This is a minimal, backward-compatible change (worktreePath
optional, old callers/tests unaffected) required to make the "existing
happy path tests still pass" acceptance criterion true once markDone is
correctly gated on merge success -- without it, the existing
"two independent tickets" and "two-wave scenario" integration tests
would fail because the second ticket's real merge would always fail.

Added 1 new integration test to test/integration.test.ts: installs a
fake `git` on PATH (same technique as test/merge.test.ts's
installMergeFailingGit) that fails `git merge <branch>` for one
specific ticket branch, runs a single ticket through runWavesIsolated
with a passing mock agent, and asserts the ticket's status stays
ready-for-agent and its worktree is left intact. Verified it fails (1
failing assertion) against the pre-fix orchestrator.ts/merge.ts and
passes after the fix.

All 294 tests pass (setup: 66, orchestrator: 72, worktree: 12,
progress: 33, merge: 38, integration: 73).
Lint clean.

