## 2026-08-29 - US-005: Implement respawn on merge conflict

Completed. Added `updateWorktreeFromBase` to src/merge.ts: runs
`git merge <baseBranch> --no-edit` inside a ticket's worktree, and
deliberately does NOT abort on failure -- conflict markers are left
in place (MERGE_HEAD still set) so the respawned agent can resolve
and commit them, matching the "update the worktree to reflect the
current base" acceptance criterion.

In src/orchestrator.ts, added `respawnAndRetryMerge` (module-private
helper) and wired it into runWavesIsolated's post-mergeWave loop: for
any branchResult with merged:false, when conflictStrategy is
'respawn' and a worktreePath is known, it now (1) merges baseBranch
into the worktree via updateWorktreeFromBase, (2) re-spawns the same
agentCmd in that worktree with a fresh `.respawn.log`, and (3) if the
respawned agent exits 0, retries with a direct `mergeBranch` (not a
second rebase -- the worktree's branch already contains base's
history via the merge in step 1, so a rebase would be redundant and
risk a second conflict path). If the respawn succeeds, the ticket is
marked done, progress is checked/committed, and its worktree is
removed via the existing US-004 cleanup path; if the respawned agent
exits non-zero or the retried merge still conflicts, the ticket is
left ready-for-agent and reported, same as the no-respawn case. This
naturally caps respawn at one attempt per ticket per wave since it
runs once inline in the per-branch loop, with no retry loop around
it.

Added 2 new integration tests to test/integration.test.ts:
- "two-branch wave, second branch conflicts, agent respawned and
  succeeds on retry": a new `installMergeFailOnceGit` fake-git helper
  (sibling to the existing installMergeFailingGit) fails `git merge
  <branch>` only on its first invocation for a given branch, then
  passes through to real git -- simulating a conflict that clears up
  after the agent "fixes" it. Two independent tickets run in one
  wave; ticket 1 merges cleanly (i===0 path), ticket 2's merge fails
  once (i>0 rebase+merge path), respawn re-runs the (always-passing)
  mock agent and the retried merge succeeds. Both tickets end up
  done and both worktrees are removed.
- "respawned agent fails, ticket stays ready-for-agent": uses a new
  stateful mock agent (test/mock-agent-once.js) that exits 0 the
  first time it's invoked in a given cwd and exits 1 on every
  subsequent invocation (tracked via a marker file written into the
  worktree), paired with the existing always-fails
  installMergeFailingGit. The initial agent run succeeds, the merge
  conflicts, respawn re-runs the same mock agent a second time in
  the same worktree -- now exiting 1 -- so the ticket stays
  ready-for-agent and its worktree is left intact.

Gotcha: a file created with the Write tool does not inherit the
executable bit, so test/mock-agent-once.js initially failed to spawn
(EACCes) and was silently caught by spawnAgent's `proc.on('error', ...)`
handler, which resolves exit code 1 -- the "respawned agent fails"
test passed for the wrong reason (the *initial* spawn failed, so
respawn was never exercised) until `chmod +x` was applied. Always
chmod +x new mock-agent-*.js fixtures and check the log output shows
the respawn path actually running, not just that the assertion passed.

Also confirmed (via a throwaway repro script) that before this fix,
a wave with one ticket merging cleanly and a second ticket's merge
staying conflicted (no respawn) would loop into a *second* wave that
tries to `git worktree add -b <branch>` the same branch/path again,
crashing with "a branch named ... already exists" -- because
respawn now resolves (or definitively fails) a conflicted ticket
within the *same* wave it occurred in, that duplicate-worktree crash
path is no longer reachable for the respawn strategy.

All 303 tests pass (setup: 66, orchestrator: 72, worktree: 12,
progress: 33, merge: 38, integration: 82).
Lint clean.

## 2026-08-29 - US-001: Fix asset resolution for npm-installed CLI

Completed. `process.argv[1]` breaks when nightralph is installed via
npm and invoked through the generated bin symlink in
node_modules/.bin/ -- depending on the platform/npm version, argv[1]
can resolve to the symlink path itself rather than the real
dist/index.js location, so `dirname(process.argv[1])` no longer
points at the directory containing the bundled skills/ and
docs-templates/ dirs. `import.meta.url` always reflects the actual
module's own location regardless of how it was invoked, so it's the
correct source of truth for asset-relative paths.

Rather than inlining `dirname(fileURLToPath(import.meta.url))`
directly in src/index.ts (which self-invokes yargs().parse() on
import and can't be unit tested per the US-001-from-original-PRD
gotcha already on record above), added a small pure helper
`getScriptDir(metaUrl:string):string` to src/resolve.ts -- the module
already holds testable CLI-adjacent logic extracted for the same
reason. index.ts now does
`const scriptDir = getScriptDir(import.meta.url)`.

Added 1 new test to test/integration.test.ts (1 assertion):
verifies getScriptDir('file:///opt/nightralph/dist/index.js')
returns '/opt/nightralph/dist'. Verified it fails against the
pre-fix resolve.ts (function did not exist) and passes after the fix.

