## 2026-09-03 - US-003: An agent that exits before reading stdin cannot crash the run

Completed. `spawnAgent` in src/orchestrator.ts wrote the prompt
directly to `proc.stdin` with no error listener. When an agent CLI
exits immediately (bad flags, auth failure) before draining stdin,
Node's write to the now-closed pipe raises EPIPE as an uncaught
`error` event on the stdin stream, which crashes the whole
orchestrator process -- taking down every other ticket's wave with
it.

Added a `proc.stdin.on('error', ...)` listener, installed before the
write, that logs the failure into the ticket's log file via
`formatErrorMessage` instead of letting it propagate. No other
behavior changes: the agent's own exit code (1, in the reproduction
case) still flows through `proc.on('close', ...)` as before, so
`spawnAgent` resolves normally with `exitCode:1, timedOut:false`.

Added test/mock-agent-exit-before-reading.js: calls `process.exit(1)`
immediately without ever registering a stdin listener, so the parent
process's write is guaranteed to hit a closed pipe.

Added one test to test/integration.test.ts
("spawnAgent - an agent that exits before reading stdin does not
crash the run") using a 2MB prompt, large enough that the OS pipe
buffer cannot absorb it in a single write so the EPIPE is reliably
triggered. Confirmed red first: running the bundled integration
suite against the pre-fix code crashed the whole test process with
an uncaught `Error: write EPIPE`, not just a single failing
assertion.

Gotcha: manually re-bundling a single test file with esbuild needs
`--packages=external` (matching the `npm test` script) -- omitting
it produces a bundle that tries to `require('stream')` at runtime
and fails immediately with a confusing "Dynamic require... not
supported" error that looks unrelated to the change under test.

Verified via `npm run build` (typecheck), `npm run lint`, the
targeted `test/integration.test.ts` bundle, and the full suite.

All 610 tests pass (setup: 79, orchestrator: 156, worktree: 25,
progress: 57, merge: 40, display: 82, testcmd: 13, integration: 158).
Lint clean.

## 2026-09-03 - US-004: The timeout kill takes the agent's process group and always resolves

Completed. Two related problems in `spawnAgent` (src/orchestrator.ts):

1. The timeout handler called `proc.kill('SIGKILL')`, which only
   kills the agent process itself. An agent that spawns a dev
   server or watcher as a child leaves that child running as an
   orphan after the timeout fires.
2. `spawnAgent`'s promise only resolved on the `close` event, which
   waits for stdout/stderr to close. If a grandchild inherited
   those pipes and outlives (or briefly outlives) the agent, `close`
   never fires and the whole ticket -- and the run -- hangs forever.

Fix: spawn agents with `detached:true` so they become their own
process group leader, and added an exported `killProcessGroup(pid)`
helper that sends `SIGKILL` to `-pid` (the whole group) instead of
just the agent. The timeout handler now calls this helper; it's
exported so the upcoming SIGINT/SIGTERM handling (US-006) can reuse
it. `spawnAgent`'s promise now also listens for `exit` (fires as
soon as the process itself dies, independent of pipe state) and
starts a 2000ms (`CLOSE_GRACE_MS`) grace timer; if `close` hasn't
already resolved the promise by then, it force-destroys
`proc.stdout`/`proc.stderr` and resolves anyway. A `settled` flag
guards against `close` and the grace timer both trying to resolve.

Added test/mock-agent-grandchild-hang.js: spawns a non-detached
child (inheriting stdout/stderr, so same process group as the
parent) that never exits on its own, writes the child's pid to
`./grandchild.pid` (relative to the mock's cwd, which the test sets
via spawnAgent's `cwd` option -- there's no existing mechanism to
pass extra argv through spawnAgent, so cwd was the simplest way to
hand the test a rendezvous path). Neither the mock nor its child
reads stdin or exits, so only the orchestrator's timeout can end
them.

Added one test to test/integration.test.ts ("spawnAgent - timeout
kills the process group..."): timeout:1, asserts `timedOut:true`,
asserts the promise resolves in well under CLOSE_GRACE_MS + a
comfortable buffer (elapsedMs < 6000; in practice ~1s, since
same-process-group SIGKILL kills the grandchild immediately and
`close` fires on its own -- the grace-timer fallback exists for the
case where a grandchild has fully detached into its own group and
survives the SIGKILL, which isn't reproduced here), and confirms via
`process.kill(grandchildPid, 0)` throwing that the grandchild
process is actually dead, not merely orphaned.

Confirmed red first: against the pre-fix code (proc.kill('SIGKILL')
+ resolve only on 'close'), the new test hung indefinitely --
running the full integration bundle with a 15s wrapper timeout
exited 124 partway through, well before reaching later tests, with
no output for the new test at all.

Gotcha: the mock script needs its executable bit set (`chmod +x`)
or spawnAgent's underlying `spawn()` call fails with EACCES, which
surfaces as `proc.on('error', ...)` and a false-looking failure
("result reports the timeout" not ok) that has nothing to do with
the process-group logic being tested.

Verified via `npm run build` (typecheck), `npm run lint`, the
targeted `test/integration.test.ts` bundle, and the full suite.

All 613 tests pass (setup: 79, orchestrator: 156, worktree: 25,
progress: 57, merge: 40, display: 82, testcmd: 13, integration: 161).
Lint clean.

## 2026-09-03 - US-005: Worktree creation is serialized

Completed. Renamed the existing `createWorktree` implementation in
`src/worktree.ts` to a private `createWorktreeNow`, and added a new
exported `createWorktree` that chains calls through a module-level
`worktreeQueue` promise: each call attaches its work with
`.then()` off the tail of the queue and immediately replaces the
tail with a `.catch(() => {})`'d copy of its own result, so a
rejected worktree creation doesn't wedge the queue for later
callers. The public signature is unchanged (still async, same
opts/return shape), so no call sites needed updates.

