## 2026-09-03 - US-010: A failed attempt's worktree is removed even when dirty

Completed. `removeWorktree` in src/worktree.ts previously ran
`git worktree remove` without `--force`, which fails when the
worktree contains modified or untracked files (git exits 128
with "contains modified or untracked files, use --force to
delete it"). Since failed agents often leave dirty files behind
(partial writes, untracked artifacts), the orchestrator's cleanup
calls silently failed and left worktree directories accumulating
as sibling directories.

Changed `removeWorktree` to accept an optional second parameter
`opts?:{ force?:boolean }`. When `opts.force` is true, `--force`
is inserted into the git args before the worktree path. The
public signature is backward-compatible (existing callers that
pass no second arg behave identically).

Updated all cleanup call sites in src/orchestrator.ts to pass
`force:true`:
- `removeWorktreeQuietly` (used after failed agent exits and
  failed test gates)
- The respawn-recovery path's direct `removeWorktree` call
- The `stoppedAt` cleanup loop's direct `removeWorktree` call

Created test/mock-agent-dirty-then-fail.js: writes an untracked
file into its cwd then exits 1 for ticket 01-*; any other ticket
succeeds like mock-agent-pass.js.

Added 2 new tests to test/worktree.test.ts:
- removeWorktree with force removes a dirty worktree
- removeWorktree without force fails on a dirty worktree

Added 1 new integration test to test/integration.test.ts:
- dirty worktree from failed agent is cleaned up: two tickets,
  01 writes dirty files and exits 1, 02 passes. Asserts 01 stays
  ready-for-agent, 02 reaches done, and 01's worktree directory
  is gone.

